 2. 🇺🇸 Milestone: $40 trillion debt binge 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: U.S. Treasury. Chart: Danielle Alberti/Axios America's national debt crossed an ominous threshold yesterday: $40 trillion — and rising by about $100 billion a week, Axios' Ben Berkowitz writes. 

 Why it matters: It's not sustainable. The inevitable reckoning means some administration, some day — perhaps sooner than later — will have to make tough choices to get the public finances in order. 🧮 By the numbers: The debt has doubled in the last 10 years and quadrupled in less than 20.

 It's the equivalent of almost $117,000 of debt for every single person in the United States. Front pages of today's Wall Street Journal, New York Times. Between the lines: It's a psychologically important milestone that underscores the real problem: the debt's incessant, rapid growth.

 Both obvious options to tackle it — spending less or taxing more — are politically treacherous. 🧐 What to watch: By some estimates , at the current pace, a $50 trillion national debt is only about six years away.

